## Why This Is Asked

Adds a practical multi-tenant privacy control layer to a beginner API, highlighting data isolation, per-tenant budgets, and simple governance in a real system.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Per-tenant privacy budgets (epsilon, delta)
- Header validation (X-tenant-id)
- Tenant-scoped configuration
- Observability and audits

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: tenant-aware config lookup (Express)
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (!tenant) return res.status(400).send('Missing X-tenant-id');
  req.tenant = loadTenantConfig(tenant); // returns {epsilon, delta, ...}
  if (!req.tenant) return res.status(403).send('Unknown tenant');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you migrate tenants to per-tenant budgets with zero downtime?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests designing a compliant, scalable, multi-tenant cca_score system with strong isolation and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via partitioned feature stores
- Per-tenant key management and encryption
- Explainability safety and PII redaction
- Immutable auditing and breach-testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: tenant-scoped scoring
function scoreForTenant(tenantId, payload, store, modelReg) {
  const part = store.getPartition(tenantId);
  const features = part.fetchFeatures(payload);
  const model = modelReg.getModel(tenantId);
  return model.predict(features);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollout under partial breach scenarios?
- How would you prove regulatory compliance to auditors?
- What metrics indicate isolation violation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical API design for client resilience and caching.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency in POST; client-side cache keys; offline mode handling; end-to-end testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class CcaClientCache {
  // simple in-memory cache for demo
  constructor(ttl=60000) {
    this.ttl = ttl; this.store = new Map();
  }
  get(key){ const v=this.store.get(key); if(!v) return null; if(Date.now()-v.t > this.ttl) { this.store.delete(key); return null; } return v.value; }
  set(key, value){ this.store.set(key, {value, t: Date.now()}); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test offline support and cache eviction?
- How would you handle multi-device sync for the same user?

